\subsection{Changepoint $\Kempirical$ Validation}
\label{sec:changepoint_validation}

To verify that $\Kempirical$ is not an artifact of the fixed threshold $\theta{=}0.60$, we apply non-parametric changepoint detection (PELT with $\ell_2$ cost, penalty $= 0.1$; BinSeg and BottomUp as robustness checks) to the pairwise AUC sequences for all 37 experiment configurations.
The changepoint-derived $\Kempirical_{\text{CP}}$ agrees with $\theta{=}0.60$-based $\Kempirical$ within $\pm 1$ for 35/37 configurations (94.6\%; exact agreement: 17/37, 45.9\%).
In all disagreement cases, the changepoint method is more conservative ($\Kempirical_{\text{CP}} \leq \Kempirical_\theta$), with disagreements occurring when boundary AUC values fall in the range $[0.607, 0.654]$.
The domain ordering (arXiv $>$ C4 $\geq$ Code $>$ Wikipedia) is preserved under the changepoint criterion.
Bootstrap resampling ($n{=}2{,}000$) confirms stability of the changepoint location.

\subsection{Benjamini-Hochberg Multiple Testing Correction}
\label{sec:bh_correction}

The method section reports permutation $p \leq 10^{-4}$ for all boundary comparisons (Section~\ref{sec:formulation}).
Benjamini-Hochberg correction across all $m{=}30$ boundary tests (10 C4 configurations $\times$ 3 boundary pairs) yields $q_{\max} = 0.003 \ll 0.05$.
Under an extreme upper bound of $m{=}185$ (all pairwise depth comparisons across all configurations), $q_{\max} = 0.019 < 0.05$.
Multiple testing therefore does not affect any $\Kempirical$ determination.

\subsection{Feature Importance Analysis}
\label{sec:feature_importance}

Permutation importance (10 repetitions, mean $\Delta\auc$) on LLaMA-3.1-8B C4 generations (Qwen-1.5B scorer) reveals a qualitative shift in discriminative features across depth boundaries (Figure~\ref{fig:feature_importance}; Table~\ref{tab:perm_importance}).
For 0v1, first-order surprisal derivative standard deviation ($\Delta^1$ Surprisal Std) accounts for 41.7\% of total importance (0.209 of 0.502 summed $\Delta\auc$), reflecting the sharp distributional shift at generation onset.
At the $\Kempirical$ boundary (1v2), the importance profile shifts: distributional spread (Surprisal Std, $\Delta\auc{=}0.058$) and second-order derivative statistics ($\Delta^2$ Surprisal Std, 0.022) dominate, while derivative features that drive 0v1 become negligible.
For 2v3, all 15 features show near-random importance ($\Delta\auc \leq 0.01$; total $\auc{=}0.532$), confirming that no individual feature retains discriminative power beyond $\Kempirical{=}2$.

